Some Members of the Assembly are puzzled by the suggestion that their title should be changed to Members of the Welsh Parliament (MWP).
This bewilderment stems from plans to re-name the Assembly to the Welsh Parliament.
Members of the entire political spectrum are concerned that this could be ridiculed.
A Member of the Assembly of the Labour Party stated that his group was concerned about rhyming with Twp and Pwp.
For readers from outside Wales: In Welsh, twp means silly and pwp means poop.
A Member of the Welsh Party Assembly commented that his entire group was "dissatisfied" and has suggested alternatives.
A member of the Welsh Conservative Party stated that his group was "open" to the change of name, but pointed out that the leap of pronunciation from MWP to muppet was small.
In this context, the pronunciation of the lyric w in Welsh lyrics is similar to that of Yorkshire's letter or English.
&quot; The Committee of the Assembly, which is currently drafting the legislation to introduce the change of name, stated: "Of course, the final decision on any descriptive element of the denomination of the Members of the Assembly will be a matter for the members themselves."
The Welsh Government's 2017 law gave the Welsh Assembly the power to change its name.
In June, the Commission published the results of a public consultation of the proposals that revealed broad support for calling the Welsh Parliament to the Assembly.
With regard to the question of the title of the Members of the Assembly, the Commission preferred Members of the Welsh Parliament (or WMP), but the MWP option obtained most of the support in a public consultation.
Assembly members appear to be suggesting alternative options, but the struggle to reach a consensus could lead to House Speaker Elin Jones, who should send the change bill within a few weeks.
The legislation on reforms will include other changes related to the functioning of the assembly, such as rules on disqualification from Assembly membership and the design of the committee system.
Members of the Assembly will have the final say in the question of what they should be called when they deliberate on legislation.
The Macedonians go to the polls to vote a referendum on the country's renaming
Voters will vote on Sunday on whether their country's name will be changed to "Republic of Northern Macedonia."
The popular vote was called in an attempt to resolve a decades-of-the-demonstage conflict with the neighboring country, Greece, which has its own province called Macedonia.
Athens has always insisted that its northern neighbour &apos; s name represents a claim from its territory and has repeatedly opposed its candidacy for membership of the EU and NATO.
The president of Macedonia, Gjorge Ivanov, who opposes the plebiscite on the change of name, has said he will ignore the vote.
But supporters of the referendum, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that the name change is simply the price that needs to be paid to join the EU and NATO.
