The Macedonians decided in a referendum on whether they are going to rename their country
The people of Macedonia decided in a referendum held on Sunday on whether their name will be changed to "Republic of Northern Macedonia," a gesture that would resolve a decades-long conflict with Greece, which had blocked its candidacy for membership of the European Union and NATO.
Greece, which has a province called Macedonia, maintains that the name of its northern neighbour is a claim of its territory and has vetoed its entry into NATO and the EU.
In June, the two governments reached an agreement on the proposed new name, but nationalist opponents argue that the change would undermine the ethnic identity of the Slavic population, majority in Macedonia.
President Gjorge Ivanov has said he will not vote in the referendum and a boycott campaign has cast doubt on whether turnout will reach the minimum 50 per cent required for the referendum to be deemed valid.
On the ballot of the referendum the following question arose: Are you in favour of accession to NATO and the EU with the acceptance of the agreement with Greece?
Supporters of the name change, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that it is a price worth paying for Macedonia, one of the countries emerging from the disintegraration of Yugoslavia, to try to gain access to bodies such as the EU and NATO.
"Today I have come to vote for the country's future, for the young people of Macedonia, so that they can live freely under the protection of the European Union, because this represents a safer life for everyone," said Olivera Georgijevska, 79, in Skopie.
Although it won't be legally binding, many Members of Parliament have said they will respect the outcome of the vote to make it decisive.
The name change requires a two-thirds majority in Parliament.
The State Electoral Commission stated that at one o'clock in the afternoon there had been no reports of irregularities.
The turnout, however, was only 16 per cent, compared with 34 per cent for the last parliamentary elections held in 2016, when 66 per cent of registered constituents cast their vote.
"I have come to vote for my children, our place is in Europe," said Gjose Tanevski, 62, who was voting in the capital, Skopie.
Macedonia's Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, his wife Zorica and his son Dushko cast their vote in Macedonia's referendum on a change of name of the country that would open the way to join NATO and the European Union in Strumica (Macedonia on September 30, 2018).
In front of Parliament in Skopie, Vladimir Kavardarkov, 54, was preparing a small stage and removing chairs in front of tents planted by those who would boycott the referendum.
"We are in favour of entering NATO and the EU, but we want to do so with our heads high, not through the back door," Kavadarkov said.
We're a poor country, but we've got dignity.
If they don't want us to come in like Macedonia, we can establish relations with other countries, such as China and Russia, and be part of Eurasian integration.
Prime Minister Zaev says accession to NATO would bring the much-needed investment Macedonia, whose unemployment rate is more than 20 per cent.
"I believe that the vast majority will be in favour, since more than 80 per cent of citizens are in favor of the EU and NATO," Zaev said after casting his vote.
He said that a positive result would be a "confirmation of our future."
A poll published last Monday by the Institute for Policy Research found that between 30 and 43 per cent of the voters were going to participate in the referendum, which represents a smaller-than-required turnout.
Another survey conducted by Telma TV of Macedonia revealed that 57 per cent of respondents planned to vote on Sunday.
Of these, 70 per cent said they would vote in favour.
For the referendum to be considered a success, turnout must be 50 per cent plus a vote.
If it fails, this would be the first serious blow to pro-Western government policy since it came to power in May last year.
