As stated in the announcement of Friday's Nottingham East Labour Party meeting, we want the meetings to be inclusive and productive.
For most of the eight years I've been a Member of Parliament of the Local Labour Party, that's exactly what Friday night meetings of the General Committee have been.
Unfortunately, this isn't the current tone of many meetings and the promise of "kinder and kinder" policy is already more than forgotten, if it was really applied at some point.
It is becoming increasingly clear that dissenting views are not tolerated in the Labour Party and that each opinion is judged on whether party leaders consider it acceptable or not.
This began shortly after Jeremy became a leader, when colleagues with whom he previously thought he shared a similar political vision they began to expect him to radically change his mind and take positions he would never have agreed with, either on national security or on the EU single market.
As long as I speak in public, no matter what he says, there's a diatribe of insults on social media demanding my retirement, denouncing the policies at the centre, telling me I shouldn't be in the Labour Party.
I'm not the only one who's experienced it.
Actually, I know I'm luckier than some of my colleagues, as the comments about me tend to be political.
I am amazed by the professionalism and determination of colleagues who face a torrent of sexist or racist insults every day, but they never run away.
One of the most disappointing aspects of this political age is the normalization of insults.
Last week, Jeremy Corbyn said the Labour Party should promote a culture of tolerance.
The reality is that we don't already have that wide range of opinions and, with every censure motion or every change in the selection rules, the match becomes narrower.
Over the last two years, I've received a lot of tips that advised me to keep my head down and don't make me heard so much and that would be nice.
But I didn't get into politics to do that.
Ever since I joined the Labour Party as a high school student, motivated by the Negligence of the Thatcher government that left the classroom of my school literally in disrepair, I've tried to support the improvement of public services for those who need them most, either as a councillor or as government minister.
I've never stoked my political ideas, either in the last election.
It's impossible for anyone in Nottingham East to be unaware of my political posturing or the areas where I didn't agree with current leaders.
The only thing I'd say to those who promoted Friday's motion is that I don't understand this desire to waste time and energy with questions about my fidelity to the leader of the Labour Party when the country is headed to a Brexit that will affect households, businesses and public services.
The real message isn't for Nottingham Momentum, it's for voters, whether it's from the Labour Party or not: I'm proud to serve you and I promise that no threat of withdrawal or any political opportunism will prevent me from acting according to what I think is best for all of you.
Chris Leslie is MP for Nottingham East
