The sea belongs to us: a landlocked Bolivia has the hope that the court will reopen the route to the Pacific
Sailors guard a naval headquarters in La Paz.
Nato-blue banners floated in public buildings.
From Lake Titicaca to the Amazes, the naval bases show the motto: "The sea belongs to us by right,"
Retrieving it is a duty.
Throughout Bolivia, a land without a sea are still vividly reminiscent of the coast lost to Chile in a bloody conflict over resources that took place in the 19th-century, in addition to the yearning to re-navigate the Pacific Ocean.
These hopes may be the greatest in decades, as Bolivia awaits a resolution of the International Court of Justice by October 1, after five years of deliberations.
"Bolivia has the impulse, a spirit of unity and serenity and, of course, it is waiting for the result with a positive vision," said Roberto Calzadilla, a Bolivian diplomat.
Many Bolivians will observe the International Court &apos; s ruling on large screens spread throughout the country, in the hope that the court in The Hague will rule in favour of Bolivia &apos; s claim that, after decades of intermittent talks, Chile will have an obligation to negotiate to grant Bolivia a sovereign exit to the sea.
Evo Morales, Bolivia's charismatic indigenous president who is waging a controversial re-selection battle next year, also has plenty to say about Monday's resolution.
"We are very close to returning to the Pacific Ocean," he promised at the end of August.
Some analysts, however, believe it is unlikely that the court will decide in favor of Bolivia and that the situation would change little if it did.
The U.N. agency based in the Netherlands does not have the power to adjudicate Chilean territory and has specified that it will not determine the outcome of possible talks.
The fact that the decision of the International Court of Justice arrives only six months after the closing arguments indicates that the case "was not complicated," said Paz Zárate, a Chilean expert in international law.
Far from promoting Bolivia's cause, the past four years may have hindered it.
"The problem of access to the sea has been kidnapped by the current Bolivian administration," Zárate said.
In addition, he suggested that Morales' belligerent rhetoric has undermined any residual Chilean goodwill.
Bolivia and Chile will continue to speak at some point, but it will be extremely difficult to hold talks after that.
The two countries haven't traded ambassadors since 1962.
Former President Eduardo Rodriguez Veltzé, Bolivia's representative in The Hague, rejected the idea that the court &apos; s decision-making was unusually fast.
On Monday it will be offered to Bolivia "an extraordinary opportunity to open a new era of relations with Chile" and the possibility of ending 139 years of disagreements with mutual benefits, he said.
Calzadilla also denied that Morales, who is still one of the most popular presidents in Latin America, used the maritime issue as a political crutch.
"Bolivia will never relinquish its right to have access to the Pacific Ocean," he added.
The resolution is an opportunity to see that we have to leave the past behind.
North Korea says there will be no nuclear disarmament unless it can rely on the U.S.
North Korean Foreign Minister Ri Yong Ho says his nation will never be the first to carry out nuclear disarmament if he can't rely on Washington.
Ri addressed the United Nations General Assembly on Saturday.
He urged the U.S. to deliver on pledges made during a summit in Singapore between rival leaders.
His comments come at a time when U.S. U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo appears to be about resuming paralyzed diplomatic nuclear talks more more than three months after the summit with North Korea's leader Kim Jong U.
Ri points out that the continuation of U.S. continued sanctions and a declaration that ends the Korean War to subjugate to the North is a "shocking."
Washington is wary to accept the declaration without Pyongyang making any meaningful disarmament moves earlier.
Both Kim and U.S. President Donald Trump want to hold another summit.
But widespread skepticism reigns about Pyongyang's seriousness of relinquishing an arsenal that the country probably sees as the only way to ensure its security.
Pompeo is scheduled to visit Pyongyang next month to prepare for another summit between the presidents.
Motion Picture parades in Paris unveil the last line of huge hats that are about to land on a shopping street near you
If you want to increase your hat collection or completely protect yourself from the sun, look no further.
