Famous stylist Luke Armitage told FEMAIL: "Next summer, I'm hoping to see huge beach hats and beach bags on the commercial streets. Since the designer has had a big impact, it would be hard to ignore the demand for over-size add-ons.
John Edward: linguistic competences are fundamental to the citizens of the world
Scotland &apos; s independent schools have a track record of academic excellence which has continued in 2018 with another set of extraordinary test results that are bolstered by individual and collective successes achieved in the fields of sport, art, music and other community projects.
With more than 30,000 pupils across Scotland, these centres, represented by the Scottish Council of Independent Colleges (SCIS), strive to deliver the best level of service to pupils and parents.
Independent schools are aimed at preparing their students to continue their higher-level studies, for the career of their choice and for their place as citizens of the world.
As an educational sector that can design and implement an adapted curriculum, it is observed that modern languages are still a popular and desired subject in the centers.
According to Nelson Mandela: "If you speak to a man in a tongue he understands, the message gets to his head.
"If you speak to him in his tongue, it comes to his heart."
It's a powerful reminder that if you want to establish relationships and build trust with people from other countries, you can't rely solely on English.
If we take into account this year's recent exam results, we can see that the languages are at the top of the qualifiers, with the highest pass rates in independent schools.
In total, 68 per cent of students who studied foreign languages obtained an A, the highest grade.
The data, collected from the 74 SCIS centres, showed that 72 per cent of the students obtained an A in Mandarin, while 72 per cent of those who studied German, 69 per cent of those who studied French and 63 per cent of those who were studying in Spanish also obtained an A.
This shows that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages because they are seen as a fundamental competence that both children and young people will undoubtedly need in the future.
At present, languages are now considered to be at the same level as CTIM (science, technology, engineering and mathematical subjects) in the curricula of independent schools and everywhere.
A survey carried out in 2014 by the Commission for Employment and Skills in the United Kingdom revealed that, of the reasons given by employers about the difficulties they encountered in filling jobs, 17 per cent were attributed to a shortage of language skills.
Language skills are therefore becoming increasingly indispensable for preparing young people for their future careers.
The possible job opportunities that require languages are on the rise, so these competences are essential in a globalized world.
Regardless of the career that someone chooses, if you've learned a second language, you will have a real advantage in the future thanks to competition like this, which will last a lifetime.
Being able to communicate directly with persons from foreign countries will allow that person to automatically get ahead of the competition.
According to a YouGov survey of more than 4,000 adults in the United Kingdom in 2013, 75 per cent of them did not speak any foreign languages well enough to have a conversation. It was also established that the only language spoken by a double-digit percentage, 15 per cent, was French.
For this reason, investing in the teaching of languages is now important for today &apos; savvy children.
If you know a number of languages, especially those in developing economies, children will be equipped to have a better opportunity to find quality employment.
In Scotland, each school will teach different languages.
Some centres will focus on more classic modern languages, while others will teach the most important languages for the United Kingdom by 2020, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
Whatever a child &apos; s interests, in independent schools you will always be able to choose between several languages taught by teaching staff specializing in this field.
Scotland's stand-alone educational centres devote all their efforts to providing a learning environment that prepares children and equips them with the skills needed to succeed, whatever the future holds for them.
At the moment it is undeniable that, in a global business environment, languages continue to be of vital importance for the country &apos; s future, so this must be reflected in education.
Modern languages should indeed be considered "competents for international communication."
Separate schools will continue to offer these options, diversity and excellence to Scotland's young people.
Il faut nice to you.
John Edward is the director of the Scottish Council of Independent Colleges
LeBron debuts with the Lakers on Sunday in San Diego
