Trump's rally time in West Virginia, YouTube channel
President Donald Trump kicks off a series of campaign rallies tonight in Wheeling, West Virginia.
It's the first of Trump's five scheduled rallies for next week, including shoes at venues with supporters such as Tennessee and Mississippi.
With his nominee's confirmation vow to fill the Supreme Court vacancy on hold, Trump aims to garner support for the up-and-coming midterm elections, as Republicans run the risk of losing congressional grip when votes are filed in November.
At what time is Trump's rally in West Virginia tonight and how do you see it online?
Trump's rally in Wheeling, West Virginia, is scheduled for 7 p.m. tonight, Saturday, September 29, 2018.
You can see it online through a live broadcast on YouTube.
Trump is likely to comment on Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh's appearances this week, which swept up because of allegations of inappropriate sexual conduct and have left an up-and-waited Senate confirmation vote on hold for at most a week as the FBI investigates them.
However, the main objective of this series of rallies is to help Republicans facing November's elections to gain momentum.
President Trump &apos; s campaign pointed out that the five rallies next this week aim to excite volunteers and supporters as Republicans try to protect and expand majorities they retain in the Senate and the House of Representatives, according to Reuters.
"Congressional control is so crucial to his agenda that the president will travel to as many states as possible as we go on the campaign trail," a Trump campaign spokesman who didn't want to give him his name told Reuters.
Scheduled at Wheeling's Wesbanco Arena Stadium, tonight's rally could engulf supporters of "Ohio and Pennsylvania," as well as have media coverage in Pittsburgh, as reported by the West Virginia Metro News.
Saturday will be Trump's second in a month to visit West Virginia, the state where he won by more than 40 percentage points in 2016.
Trump's trying to help West Virginia's Republican Senate nominee Patrick Morrisey who is lagging behind in the polls.
"It's not a good sign that the president will have to come and give him a push in the polls," Simon Haeder, political scientist at the University of West Virginia, told Reuters.
Ryder Cup 2018: The U.S. team says he dares to fight to keep alive the hope for Sunday's individual games.
After three individual sessions, Saturday afternoon's foursomes may be what this Ryder Cup needed.
Impulse oscillating pendulum is a fully invented sporting concept, but players really believe in it, even more in competitions like this.
Then where would you say the impulse is right now?
"They took us a six-point lead and now they've got us four, so I suppose they can be considered a small impulse," Jordan Spieth said at the end of the day.
Europe has an advantage, of course: four points ahead with twelve more at stake.
As Spieth points out, U.S. people feel they've got some headwind in favor and plenty of mood to be cherished, among other reasons for the pair formed by Spieth and Justin Thomas, who played together all day and got three points out of four each.
Spieth's been lethal from tee to the green and preaches by example.
Glorious screams of celebratory rose as his round progressed, introducing a vital putt to tying the fourth game when he and Thomas went two down after two holes.
The putt that gave them the game in the 15th was greeted with a similar screamery, of those who convey that he believes the U.S. team hasn't been left out yet.
"That has to be redoubled and concentrate on the game," Spieth said.
That's what these players have done.
Eighteen holes to leave a mark.
The only players with more points than Spieth and Thomas in the past two days are Francesco Molinari and Tommy Fleetwood, the undisputed Ryder Cup tally.
The bizarre and adorable couple in Europe carries four out of four and nothing can go wrong.
It was the only couple who didn't make a bogey on Saturday afternoon, but they also avoided bogeys on Saturday morning, Friday afternoon and on the last nine holes on Friday morning.
That streak and the way its energy flows with the bustling audience makes it clear that these are the players you have to beat on Sunday, and that there will be no other players as popular as Fleetwood and Molinari to seal a possible European victory when the sun sets at Le Golf National.
Prefessibly, at the same time in different holes.
Even so, it remains premature to talk about European glory.
Bubba Watson and Webb Simpson dispatched Sergio Garcia, the hero of the morning's four-wilves, after forming a match with Alex Noren.
A bogey and two-doubles in the first nine holes buried the Spaniard and the Swede in a hole they couldn't leave.
But on Sunday there won't be any help getting out of the holes.
4balls and foursomes are fascinating to see them up close because you can appreciate the interactions between couples, the tips they give and those they don't give, and the way a strategy can change in an instant.
So far, Europe has played better as a team and carries a big advantage for the last day, but this foursomes session also showed that the U.S. team dares to fight, something that some, especially the Americans, were hesitating.
Europe advances by 10-6 the last day of the Ryder Cup
Europe departs with a good advantage on the final day of the Ryder Cup, after finishing the fourballs and foursomes with a 10-stage result over the United States.
Tommy Fleetwood-built duo and Francesco Molinari led contention with two victories over a Tiger Woods who looked in trouble, leading by four points in the Le Golf National qualifiers.
European Thomas Bjorn, trying to retain the truffled trophy they lost at Hazeltine two years ago, dominated an U.S. team that made plenty of mistakes during the morning's fourballs, who finished 3-1.
The U.S. offered more endurance in the foursomes, winning by two games, but failed to shorten distances.
U.S. Jim Furyk needs to get eight points from Sunday's 12 individual games to retain the trophy.
Fleetwood is Europe's first rookie winning four consecutive points. In addition, he and Molinari, who were nicknamed Molliwood after a sensational weekend, they are the second pair to win four points in their first four games in Ryder Cup history.
After beating Woods and Patrick Reed at the Fourballs, their magnificent junction served to beat disgraced Woods and American rookie Bryson Decahmbeau by a resounding 5-4.
Woods, who in Saturday's two matches hadn't excelled, had occasional brilliance moments, but has now lost 19 of his 29 four-day and four-way games, and seven games in a row.
Justin Rose, who was knocked out for the morning's fourballs, was again Henrik Stenson's partner in the foursomes, beating Dustin Johnson and Brooks Koepka, number one and number three in the world respectively, with a 2-1 result.
Although the day was pleasant and quiet in the south-west of Paris, Europe didn't have everything on its part.
The three-time streak, Jordan Spieth, and Justin Thomas served as benchmarks for the Americans, garnering two points on Saturday.
They secured an exciting 2-1 victory against Ian Poulter and Spaniard Jon Rahm in the fourballs and then beat Poulter and Rory McIlroy by 4-3 in the foursomes after losing the opening two holes.
In Ryder Cup history that a team recovered from a four-point gap in individual matches has only happened twice, although Furyk, as a stunt holder, only needs a junction to retain it.
Although it's been the second-best for two days, it looks like carrying out a counter-attack on Sunday is beyond your chances.
North Korea says there's no chance they'll be dismantled first unilaterally if they don't have confidence
North Korea's foreign minister informed the United Nations on Saturday that the continuation of the sanctions deepened its mistrust in the U.S. and that there was no possibility that his country would renounce nuclear weapons unilaterally under those circumstances.
Ri Yong Ho stated before the annual General Assembly of the international organization that North Korea had taken "important measures of goodwill" over the past year, such as stopping nuclear and missile tests, dismantling nuclear test facilities and committing to the non-proliferation of nuclear weapons and nuclear technology.
"However, we don't get a similar response from the United States," he said.
If we don't have confidence in the United States, we can't have confidence about our national security, and, in those circumstances, there's no chance that we'll first unilaterally disengage.
Although Ri echoed North Korea's well-known complaints about Washington &apos; s resistance to a denuclearization formula "by-studio" that rewards the country as it takes gradual steps, its statement is important, since it simply rejected a unilateral denuclearization, just as Pyongyang has done in the past.
Ri referred to a joint statement issued by Kim Jong Un and Donald Trump after the first summit between an active U.S. president and a North Korean leader on June 12 in Singapore, when Kim pledged to work for the "denuclearization of the Korean peninsula" and Trump guaranteed North Korea's security.
North Korea has chased the official end of the 1950-53 Korean War, but the U.S. has said that Pyongyang must renounce its nuclear weaponry sooner.
Washington has also resisted calls to soften international sanctions on North Korea.
"The U.S. has insisted on denuclearization in the first place and has increased the pressure through sanctions in order to achieve their purpose in a coercive manner, even objecting to a declaration of termination of the war, Ri said.
The idea that the sanctions can cause us to fall on our knees is a chimera of people who don't know us.
The problem is that the continuation of the sanctions deepens our mistrust.
Ri made no mention of the plans for a second summit between Kim and Trump, announced hype and saucer by the U.S. leader at the United Nations earlier this week.
On the other hand, the minister highlighted three meetings held between Kim and Moon Jae, South Korea's leader, over the past five months, adding: "If South Korea had been the interested party in denuclearization instead of the United States, the denuclearization of the Korean peninsula would not be at such a stalemate."
Even so, the tone of Ri's speech was radically different from last year &apos; s address, when he told the U.N. General Assembly that it was inevitable to direct North Korea's missiles towards U.S. soil after the Equirty President Trump, on a suicidal mission, called Kim a rocket man.
This year at the United Nations, Trump, who last year has threatened to destroy North Korea altogether, praised Kim for his bravery in progress towards disarmament, but said there was still a lot to be done and there was a need to continue to implement sanctions until North Korea unnuclearized.
On Wednesday, Trump stated that there was no established deadline for this: "It can take two years, three years, or five months, it doesn't matter."
China-Russia allege the U.N. Security Council should reward Pyongyang for its progress.
However, just as U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo informed the U.N. Security Council on Thursday: "The imposition of Security Council sanctions must continue vigorously and non-defense until we realize the definitive and fully verified denuclearization."
The Security Council has unanimously imposed sanctions on North Korea since 2006, in an attempt to cut funding for Pyongyang's ballistic and nuclear missile programs.
Pompeo rallied with Ri outside the U.N. General Assembly and later said he would visit Pyongyang again next month to prepare for a second summit.
Pompeo has already visited North Korea three times this year, but his last trip didn't go well.
He left Pyongyang in July saying that there had been advances, but, a few hours later, North Korea denounced that its "requirements were those of a mobster."
At a meeting with Moon earlier this month, North Korea pledged to dismantle a missile site and a nuclear complex if the U.S. implemented the corresponding measures.
He said that Kim had informed him that the corresponding measures he was looking for were the security guarantees Trump had promised in Singapore and steps towards normalizing relations with Washington.
Harvard Students Take a Course on How to Remnant Enough
Thanks to a new course being taught this year at Harvard University, all university students sleep more in an attempt to combat the growing macho culture of studying all-the-clo night drinking caffeine.
A professor discovered that students at the world's number one university usually have no idea about basic self-care.
Charles Czeisler, professor of sleep medicine at Harvard School of Medicine, and a specialist at Brigham and Women's Hospital designed the course, is believed to be the first class in the U.S.
His motivation to start the course arose after giving a chat about how sleep deprivation affects learning.
At the end of the course, a girl approached me and asked me why she just found out about this now, in her last year.
"He said that no one had ever talked to him about the importance of the dream, which surprised me," he told The Telegraph.
The course, given for the first time this year, explains to students the basic concepts of how good sleep habits contribute to academic and athletic performance, in addition to improving general well-being.
Paul Barreira, professor of psychiatry at the Harvard School of Medicine and executive director of the university's health services, noted that the university decided to introduce the course after discovering that students barely slept during the week.
The one-hour-long course includes a series of interactive tasks.
In one section, there's an image of a bedroom, where students click coffee cups, curtains, sneakers and books that inform them of the effects of caffeine and light, as well as the impact of lack of sleep on sports performance and the importance of establishing a routine to go to sleep.
In another section, participants are informed how long-term sleep deprivation can increase the risk of infarction, embolism, depression and cancer.
Later, a campus icon with interactive icons encourages participants to think about their daily routines.
Do you know it won't change student behavior instantly.
However, we believe that they have the right to know it, just as anyone has the right to know the health effects of tobacco smoking, added Professor Czeisler.
He stated that the culture of pride to spend the night sailing - it still exists and added that modern technology and the increasing pressure to which the students are subjected meant that sleep deprivation was a growing problem.
Sleeping long enough and a good-quality dream should be the secret weapon of the students to combat stress, tiredness and anxiety; even to avoid gaining weight, since sleep deprivation causes the brain to generate a sense of starvation, so they want to eat all the time.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old Californian who studies chemical and physic physics biology, helped Professor Czeisler design the course after attending one of his classes the previous year during his first year at Harvard.
He pointed out that the course had opened his eyes and was inspired to extend it to the entire campus.
He hopes that the next step will be to ask all graduate students to complete a similar curriculum before joining the competitive institution.
Professor Czeisler &apos; s recommendation is for students to think about setting an alarm to go to bed and one to wake up, and to be aware of the harmful effects of the blue light emitted the screens of electrical appliances and LEDs, which can alter the circadian rhythms, so they would end up having trouble falling asleep.
Livingston 1 - Rangers 0: Menga goal knocks Gerrard's men
The Rangers endured another episode of depression away, when Dolly Menga's attack banished Steven Gerrard's disheartening team to a 1-0 defeat at Livingston.
Ibrox wanted to secure their first home win since February's 4-1 triumph at St. Johnstone, but Gary Holt's team knocked out Gerrard for the second time in 18-game games as a coach, leaving his team to eight points from the Hearts, unleagued Ladbrokes Premiership leaders.
Menga's attack came minutes before half-time and Rangers' uninspired line-up didn't live up at any point.
The Rangers are now down to the sixth, the Livingston rise to the third, behind Hibernian because of the goal difference.
The Rangers may face more problems, as line judge Calum Spence had to receive medical assistance for a head injury after someone allegedly threw an object from the visiting background.
Gerrard switched to eight headliners who won the match against Ayr, entering the semi-finals of the Scottish League Cup.
Holt, meanwhile, pulled the 11 Liveston players who booted the Hearts a point last week and would have rejoiced at the way his organising team drowned the opponents at all times.
The Rangers may have had more possession, but Liveston took better advantage of the ball when they had it.
They should have scored within two minutes of kick-off when Menga made a pass into space for Scott Pittman that passed between the defence, leaving him alone against Allan McGregor, but the midfielder sent it away.
Keaghan Jacobs pulled off a foul-in-in-depth for captain Craig Halket, but his team-mate in defence, Alan Lithgow, was only able to send it out for the long pole.
The Rangers regained their grip, but there seemed to be more hope than conviction about their game in the last third.
There is no doubt Alfredo Morelos felt he had been penalized in the 15th minute of the game when he and Steven Lawless collided, but referee Steven Thomson echoed deaf at the Colombian's request.
The Rangers got two shots behind in the first half, but former Ibrox goalkeeper Liam Kelly barely found himself struggling to grapple with Lassana Coulibaly and Ovie Ejaria's boring pitch.
