Almanac: The inventor of the Geiger accountant
And now, a page from the Almanac section of "Sunday Morning": September 30, 1882, today 136 years ago, and CONTANDO... was born in Germany the physicist future Johannes Wilhelm "Hans" Geiger.
Geiger developed a method of detecting and measuring radioactivity, an invention that culminated in the device known as the Geiger accountant.
The accountant Geiger, who has since been a pillar of science, also became a mainstay of pop culture, such as Coronado's bell film "The Bells of Coronado," with actors Roy Rogers and Dale Evans in the seemingly incompatible roles of cowboy scientists:
Man: What the hell is that?
Rogers: It's a Geiger meter used to locate radioactive minerals, such as uranium.
By putting on your headphones, you can hear the effect of ripples emitted by the radioactivity of the minerals.
Evans: It's like a radius.
Geiger died in 1945 when he was about to turn 63.
But the inconvenience that bears his name is still alive.
A new Cancer Vacation May Teach The Immune System to The Mood Cells
A new anti-canc cancer vaccine can teach the immune system the malignant cells and kill them.
As part of the treatment, the vaccine teaches the immune system to recognize malignant cells.
The method requires the extraction of immune cells from a patient and altering them in the lab.
Thus, they can see a protein common to many cancers and re-enact them.
A study of the vaccine is generating promising results in patients with different cancers.
A woman dealing with the vaccine, which teaches the immune system to recognize malignant cells, saw their ovarian cancer disappear for more than 18 months.
The method requires the removal of immune cells from a patient and altering them in the lab so that they can "discover a protein common to many cancers, called HER2, and then reinject the cells.
Professor Jay Berzofsky, of the U.S. National Cancer Institute in Bethesda, Maryland, commented: "Our results suggest that we have a very promising vaccine."
HER2 promotes the growth of different types of cancer, such as breast, ovary, lung or colorectal cancer, Professor Berzofsky explained.
A resembling approach to taking immune cells from patients and "teaching them," attacking cancer cells has worked in the treatment of a type of leukemia.
