HIV/AIDS: China reports 14 per per cent more new cases
China has announced an increase of 14 per cent in the number of citizens living with HIV and AIDS.
There are more than 820 000 people affected in the country, according to the health authorities.
In the second quarter of 2018 alone, some 40,000 new cases were reported.
The vast majority of these were sexually assaulted, which represents a change from the past.
Traditionally, HIV spread rapidly throughout parts of China as a result of infected blood transfusions.
However, the number of people contracting HIV in this way has been reduced almost entirely, Chinese health authorities said at a congress held in Yunnan province.
Each year the number of people living with HIV and AIDS in China increases by about 100,000 people.
Transmission of HIV by sex is a serious problem in China's LGTB community.
Homosexuality decriminalized in China in 1997, but the LGTB collective suffers a great deal of discrimination.
Because of the country's Conservative values, studies have calculated that between 70 per cent and 90 per cent of men who have sexual intercourse with other men will end up marrying women.
Many of the transmissions of the diseases are because they do not use adequate protection in these relationships.
Since 2003, China's government has vowed universal access to anti-HIV drugs as part of an attempt to tackle the problem.
Maxine Waters denies that a member of his staff leaked data from senators of the Republican Party and asserts that these are dangerous woes and conspiratorial theories.
Rep. Maxine Waters on Saturday criticized accusations that a member of his staff had published the personal data of three U.S. senators of the Republican Party on the Wikipedia pages about legislators.
The L.A. Democrat stated that these accusations were prompted by commentators and websites of "extreme right."
"Lesters, lies and more vile lies," Waters said in a Twitter post.
Supposedly, the published information included the addresses and telephone numbers of U.S. senators Lindsey Graham of South Carolina and Mike Lee and Orrin Hatch, both from Utah.
On Thursday, an unfamiliar Capitol man posted the information online during a session of the senator's panel regarding allegations of inappropriate sexual conduct against Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh.
The leak occurred at some point after the three senators had interrogated Kavanaugh.
Conservative Sites, such as Gateway Pundit and RedState, reported that the IP address that identifies the origin of the publications was associated with Waters' office and made public the data of a Waters staff member, according to the Capitol.
"This unsubstantiated accusation is utterly fake and an absolute lie," Waters continued.
The member of my staff, whose identity, personal data and security have been affected as a result of these fraudulent and false accusations, was not at all responsible for leaking this information.
This unsubstantiated claim is utterly untrue and an utter bliquee.
Waters' statement sparked immediate criticism on the Internet, including that of former White House press secretary Ari Fleischer.
"He denies this angry information," Fleischer wrote.
This suggests not the temperance necessary to be a Member of Congress.
When someone is accused of something they didn't, they shouldn't get angry.
You mustn't revolt.
You mustn't question the accuser's motives.
Must keep calm and serenity.
Fleischer reportedly wanted to compare Waters' reaction to Democrats" criticism of Judge Kavanaugh, whom detractors accused of appearing too angry during Thursday's appearance.
Omar Navarro, a Republican nominee who intends to unseat Waters in the midterm elections, also expressed his opinion on Twitter.
"If it's true, it is very serious," he tweeted.
In his statement, Waters said that his office had alerted the relevant authorities and the law enforcement entities of these fraudulent charges.
"We will make sure that the authors are revealed," he continued, "and that they are considered legally responsible for all these destructive and dangerous actions for all members of my staff."
