What is "Global Citizen Festival"? Have you managed to reduce poverty?
This Saturday, New York will host the Global Citizen Festival, an annual music festival, where a stunning star poster for an equally stunning mission: to end poverty in the world.
This year, in its seventh edition, the Global Citizen Festival will see tens of thousands of people travel to the Great Lawn of Central Park to enjoy performances such as Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but also to raise awareness of the actual goal of the event: ending extreme poverty by 2030.
The Global Citizen Festival, which began in 2012, is an extension of the Global Poverty Project, an international awareness-raising group that hopes to end poverty by increasing the number of people actively battling it.
If you would like to receive a free ticket for the event (unless you can afford a VIP ticket), concert attendees have had to complete a number of tasks or actions, such as volunteering, sending e-mails to a world leader, making a phone call or anything else to raise awareness about their goal of ending poverty.
However, what has the Global Citizen achieved with 12 years of achieving its goal?
The idea of rewarding with a free gig is a genuine way to persuade people to take action or isn't more than a form of so-called "clictivism," which's called the posture we adopt when we think we're doing something really useful when signing a petition online or sending a tweet?
Since 2011, Global Citizen claims to have recorded more than 19 million "shares" of its supporters in favor of a large number of diverse targets.
Thanks to these actions, they claim to get world leaders announcing commitments and policies worth more than $37bn, which will impact the lives of more than 2250 million people by 2030.
At the beginning of 2018, the group cited 390 commitments and advertisements that arose from its shares, of which at least $10 billion has already been disbursed or raised.
The group estimates that guaranteed funds have directly influenced nearly 649 million people around the world.
Among the most important commitments is "The Power of Nutrition," an association of UK-based investors and executors united with the common commitment to "help children grow up and develop with their full potential." To this end, it has been proposed to provide Rwanda with $35 million to help end the country's malnutrition after receiving more than 4,700 tweets from Global Citizens collaborators.
"Thanks to the support of the United Kingdom government, the donors, the national governments and collaborators of Global Citizens like you, we can mark a small milestone in the history of the social injustice of undernutrition," Tracey Ullman, an ambassador for "The Power of Nutrition," told the crowd during a concert in April 2018 in London.
The group also stated that, having carried out more than 5,000 actions to try to improve nutrition in the United Kingdom for mothers and children, the government announced the funding of a project, Power of Nutrition, with which 5 million women and children will benefit from nutrition-related interventions.
In response to one of the frequently asked questions on its website about what we believe can be asked to remedy extreme poverty,
A Global Citizen collaborator replied: "It's going to be a long and tough road and, on many occasions, stumbling and fall.
But, as the human rights and anti-apartheid movements have previously achieved, we will achieve our goals, because we are stronger together.
Janet Jackson, The Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B and Janelle Monáe are some of the performances we'll feature at this year's New NY event, which will be presented by Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman.
U.S. could use navy to carry out blockade of Russia's energy exports - Interior Secretariat
"By necessary," Washington could turn to its Navy to prevent Russian energy from shattering markets, including the Middle East, U.S. Interior Secretary Ryan Zinke revealed, according to the Washington Examiner.
Zinke argued that Russia's alliance with Syria, particularly where it operates at the behest of the legitimate government, is nothing more than a pretext for expansion in new markets.
"I am convinced that they are in the Middle East to negotiate with energy, just as they do with Eastern Europe, becoming the southernmost supplier in Europe," he said.
In addition, according to official information, there are forms and methods of dealing with this issue.
"The U.S., with its Navy, has the ability to ensure sea lanes remain open and, if necessary, they could even be blocked to ensure energy doesn't end up on the market," he said.
Zinke addressed people who attended an event organized by the Consumer Energy Alliance, a non-for-for-profit group that defines itself as the "energetic consumer voice" in the United States.
In it, he likened Washington &apos; s approaches in its negotiations with Russia and Iran, and cautioned that they are, in fact, the same.
"The economic alternative in Iran and Russia basically consists of taking advantage of and replacing fuels," he warned at the same time indicating that Russia only knows how to do one thing, which is to base its economy on fossil fuels.
These claims come as the Trump administration has been attempting to boost the export of its liquefied natural gas to Europe and releave Russia, which is the cheapest option for European consumers.
To do this, members of the Trump administration, including Donald Trump himself, are trying to persuade Germany to abandon the "inappropriate" Nord Stream 2 pipeline project, which Trump says will make Berlin hostage to Moscow.
Moscow has insisted at all times that the Nord Stream 2 pipeline project, which is worth $11bn and is expected to double the pipeline's current capacity to 110 billion cubic meters a purely-in-expensive project.
The Kremlin argues that Washington's fierce opponent of the project responds solely to economic reasons and is yet another sign of unfair competition.
"I think we all share the idea that energy can't become a pressure tool and that consumers should be able to choose their suppliers," Russian Energy Minister Aleksandr Novak said after holding a meeting with U.S. Energy Secretary Rick Perry last September in Moscow.
The U.S. position, however, has met with the rejection of Germany, which has reaffirmed its commitment to the project.
The organization that leads the industry in Germany, the Federation of German Industries (BDI), has called on the U.S. not to intervene in EU energy policy or bilateral agreements between Berlin and Moscow.
"It's a big problem when a third state interferes with our energy supply," said Dieter Kempf, director of the Federation of German Industries (BDIs) following a recent meeting between German Chancellor Angela Merkel and Russian President Vladimir Putin.
