The U.S. ambassador accuses China of "bullying" with "propaganda"
It comes a week after an official Chinese newspaper published a four-page announcement in a U.S. diary newspaper promoting the mutual benefits of U.S.-China trade, the U.S. ambassador to China accused Beijing of using the U.S. press to spread its propaganda.
U.S. President Donald Trump on Wednesday made reference to China Daily's payment supplement on the Des Moines Register, Iowa's most-selling newspaper, after accusing China of wanting to meddle in the U.S. congressional election on November 6, something China flatly denies.
Trump &apos; shooter about Beijing's attempt to sway the U.S. elections marked what U.S. officials told Reuters as a new phase in a growing campaign launched by Washington to put pressure on China.
While it is common for foreign governments to publish advertisements to promote their commercial activities, Beijing and Washington are currently locked in a growing trade war that has led them to raise tariff levels on imports by both sides.
China's countervailing tariffs that applied at the beginning of the trade war were meant for them to reciprocate exporters from states such as Iowa, which supported Trump's Republican Republican Party, Chinese and U.S. experts said.
Terry Branstad, the U.S. U.S. ambassador to China and the former governor of Iowa, who is a major exporters of agricultural products to China, said Beijing had harmed U.S. workers, farmers and entrepreneurs.
As Branstad wrote in an op-ed in Sunday's Des Moines Register, China "is further intensifying that pressure with advertisement publications in our free press."
"To try to spread its propaganda, China's government relies on America's cherished tradition of freedom of expression and free press by attempting to publish a pay ad on the Des Moines Register," Branstad wrote.
"Whether at the street kiosk where we're here in Beijing, you only see a few dissenting voices and no real reflection of the disparate opinions that people in China may have about the troubling trajectory the country's economy is taking, as the media is controlled by strong pressure from the Chinese Communist Party," he wrote.
He added that "one of China's most relevant newspapers evaded the offer to publish" his article, although he did not specify which newspaper it was.
Analysts say Republicans are distancing themselves from women ahead of mid-pre-preliant voting with Kavanaugh's debacle
Since there are many renowned Republicans who are closing ranks for Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh in the face of various allegations of sexual assault, Analysts have warned that there may be a setback, especially on the part of women, in the up-and-the-half-term vote.
The emotions that surround this fact have been very intense and there are many Republicans who have stated that they want to go ahead with the vote.
"It's not possible to back down on these kinds of issues," Grant Reeher, a professor of political science at the Maxwell School of Syracuse, told The Hill in an article that was published on Saturday.
Mr. Reeher said hesitating that Sen. Jeff Flake (R-Arizona)'s latest attempt to stand in favor of the FBI investigation is enough to allay displeased voters on the issue.
"Women are not going to forget what happened yesterday; they won't forget it tomorrow or in November," Karine Jean-Pierre, a senior adviser and national spokeswoman for the MoveOn-progressive group said Friday, according to the Washington, D.C. newspaper.
On Friday in the morning, the protesters who gathered at the gates of the Senate chanted "November is coming" (November will arrive soon) at a time when Republicans who controlled the Judicial Committee decided to move forward with Kavanaugh's candidacy, despite the testimony of Dr. Christine Blasey Ford, according to Mic.
"Democrats' enthusiasm and motivation is out of every prognosis," Stu Rothenberg, a non-partisan political analyst, told the news site.
"People say the repercussion is already very relevant and, indeed, that's right.
But it could be even greater, especially among undecided women in the outdoor neighborhoods and younger voters, aged between 18 and 29, who don't usually vote even if they don't like the president."
Even before Ford's public testimony in which his allegations of sexual abusing against the Supreme Court nominee, analysts indicated there could be a setback if the Republicans went ahead with the confirmation.
"This issue has become a big mess for the Republican Party," said Michael Steele, the former chairman of the Republican National Committee, early last week, according to NBC News.
"It's no longer just a matter of the committee's vote or the final vote, it's not even if they feel Kavanaugh on the bench; it's also about the way Republicans have handled all this and how they've treated it," Guy Cecil, director of Priorities USA (a group that helps in the election of Democrats) told the news channel.
However, the Americans seem to be divided; they don't know who they should believe after the testimonies of Ford and Kavanaugh, although there are a few more who side with the latter.
A new YouGov poll indicates that 41 per cent of respondents believe Ford's testimonials definitively or likely, while 35 per cent claim to believe Kavanaugh definitively or likely.
In addition, 38 per cent stated that they thought that, in all probability or definitively, Kavanaugh has lied during his testimony, while 30 per cent said the same about Ford.
Following Flake &apos; s push, the FBI is currently investigating Ford &apos; s statements, as well as at least those of another whistleblower, Deborah Ramirez, The Guardian reported.
Ford testified under oath before the Senate Judiciary Committee last week and said Kavanaugh raped her while drunk at the age of 17.
For his part, Ramirez allege that the Supreme Court nominee showed him his genitals at a party when he was studying at Yale in the 1980s.
The inventor of the World Wide Web intends to launch a new Internet to compete with Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the developer of the World Web, is launching a new company that tries to rival Facebook, Amazon and Google.
Inrupt is the latest tech legend project - a company based on Berners-Lee's Solid open-source platform.
Solid allows users to choose where to store their data and which people will have access to what information.
In an up-to-flopping interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee joked that the intention behind Inrupt is to "dominate the world."
"It's got to be right now," he said of the startup.
"It's a historic moment".
The app uses Solid's technology so that users can create their own "on-the-on-line personal data storage" or POD.
It may contain lists, to-do lists, the calendar, the music library and other personal and professional tools.
It's like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify are all available in a browser at the same time.
Something that's unique in the storage of personal online data is that it's the user who fully chooses who can access what type of information.
The company calls it "personal training through the data."
The idea of Inrupt, according to the company's CEO, John Bruce, is to incorporate resources, processes and the skills necessary for Solid to reach the entire world.
At the moment, the company is formed by Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security brace IBM bought, some of the developers included in staff who were hired to work on the project and a community of voluntary programmers.
Starting this week, tech developers from around the world would be able to create their own devolved apps with the tools available on Inrupt's website.
Berners-Lee said that he and his team aren't talking to "Facebook and Google about whether or not they're going to introduce a radical change where all of their company models are fully modified overnight.
"We won't ask permission".
In a publication on Medium on Saturday, Berners-Lee wrote about Inrupt that "your mission is to provide commercial energy and an ecosystem to help protect the integrity and quality of the new website created in Solid."
In 1994, Berners-Lee transformed the Internet when he founded the World Wide Web Consortium (W3C) at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In recent months, Berners-Lee has played a hugely influential role in debating net neutrality.
Despite even pitching Inrupt, Berners-Lee will continue to be the founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
"I'm unbelievably optimised about this upcoming web age," Berners-Lee added.
Bernard Vann: clergyman who won a Victoria Cross in World War I
The only clergyman of the Church of England to win a Victoria Cross during World War I as a fighter has been honored in his hometown 100 years later.
Reverend Lieutenant Colonel Bernard Vann obtained this decoration on September 29, 1918 in the Bellenglise and Lehaucourt attack.
However, a sniper killed him four days later and never knew he had been awarded the greatest British military honour.
On Saturday, his two grandchildren unveiled a memorial stone at a parade in Rushden, Northamptonshire.
One of them, Michael Vann, said it was "out of a special symbolism" to unveil the stone exactly 100 years after the battle that made his grandfa grandfather deserved the decoration.
According to the London Gazette on September 29, 1918, Lt. Col. Vann guided his battalion through the Saint-Quentin Canal "travelling a thick fog and under the intense fire of machine guns and canyons."
He then rushed to the line of fire and, with "greater bravery," advanced the line before reaching a one-hand cannon and knocking down three of the detachment.
Lieutenant Colonel Vann was killed by a German sniper on October 4, 1918, just a month after the war ended.
Michael Vann, 72, said his grandfather &apos; s actions were "so great that I will never be able to reach his height, but they were very instructive."
He and his brother, Dr. James Vann, also deposited a wreath of flowers after the parade, which was attended by the Brentwood band Imperial Youth Band.
Michael Vann said it "was a privilege for him to be able to be part of the parade" and added that "the value of an authentic hero is evident when he's shown such a large endorsement."
MMA followers were awake all over the night for the Bellator 206 but only saw Peppa Pig
Imagine he's staying up all night to watch the entire Bellator 206 so they don't let him see the main event in the end.
The San Jose poster had 13 scrambles, six included in the main card, and in the United Kingdom it was to be broadcast live in the evening on Channel 5.
By 6pm, just as Gegard Mousasi and Rory MacDonald were preparing to face each other, UK viewers stunned when the show switched to Peppa Pig.
Some didn't be surprised after they had to stay awake until early in the morning only to watch the fight.
A follower on Twitter described the switch to cartoons as "a heavy joke."
"There's a government regulation that regulates the content that's aired from 6 a.m. and it wasn't appropriate, so they had to switch to children's programming," said Dave Schwartz, Bellator's senior vice president of marketing and communication, when asked about the broadcast.
""Pep the pig," yeah".
Bellator company president Scott Coker said they will modify the program to include British spectators in the upcoming editions.
"When I'm thinking about the retransmission option, I think it can work," Coker said.
"But it's at 6 a.m. on Sunday, so we wouldn't be able to do it until Sunday at our time, which would already be Monday for them.
We're watching how to do it.
Believe me, when the programming was changed, a large number of messages were received and not everyone was nice, precisely.
We've been trying to fix it, 'cause at first we thought it was a technical problem.
But it wasn't, it was a government problem.
I can promise next time it won't happen.
We'll reduce it to five fights rather than six, which we've usually done - we've tried to offer more than usual so the fans were happy and we went through.
It's an unlucky situation.'
Desert Island Discs: Tom Daley Feel Less for His Sexuality
Olympians Tom Daley says he grew up feeling inferior to each other because of his sexuality, although this was, in turn, what motivated him to become a winner.
At the age of 24, he said he didn't realize it until he got into high school that "they weren't everyone like me."
On Lauren Lauren Laverne's first Desert Island Discs show, she said her intention when talking about gay rights was to transmit "hope" to other people.
He also tells us that becoming a father made him begin to downplay the winning event at the Olympics.
The usual presenter of this legendary show, Kirsty Young, will be absent for a few months due to illness.
In Laverne's first program, Daley, who showed himself as an outcast, commented that he felt "inferior" to the rest during his childhood, because "it was not socially accepted that you liked boys and girls."
He said: "Until today, that sense of inferiority and being different has really given me the power and strength to succeed."
He wanted to show that he was "something," he said, so he didn't disappoint when he finally discovered his sexuality.
The winner-and-two bronze medals at the Olympics has become a leading profile of the LGBT movement and its image has been used at this year's Commonwealth Games held in Australia to demand that more countries decriminalise homosexuality.
He said he counted it because he felt lucky to live in freedom, without reprisal, and he wanted to convey that "hope" to other people.
The three-time world champion declared that for him it was also quite a surprise to fall in love with a man, U.S. filmmaker Dustin Lance Black, whom he met in 2013.
Daley was married last year to an Oscar-winner for 20 years, although she specified that the age difference had never been a problem.
"When you've lived so much from so young" (he went to his first Olympics at the age of 14 and his father died of cancer three years later), it's hard to find someone of the same age who's gone through the same talls and lows as you.
The couple had a son in June who was named by Robert Ray Black-Daley and Daley said their "global perspective" had changed.
"If I'd been asked last year, every answer would have revolved around 'I have to win a gold medal,'" he said.
"But he knows what, there's more important things than the Olympic gold medals.
My Olympian-gold medal is certainly Robbie."
His son is named as his father, Robert, who died in 2011 at the age of 40 after he was diagnosed with brain cancer.
Daley said his father didn't accept that he was going to die and, in fact, one of the last things he asked was if they had the passes for London 2012, because he wanted to be in the front row.
"I wasn't able to tell him I wouldn't be in the front row," he said.
"He had his hand between mine when he stopped breathing and it wasn't until that very moment when he stopped breathing and died that I realised that he wasn't invincible," he said.
The following year, Daley competed at the 2012 Olympics and won the bronze medal.
"At the time I knew that's what I've dreamed of all my life: racing in the swimming swimming pool in front of mine at an Olympics, there wasn't any better," he said.
This fact was also the one that inspired him when it came to choosing his first song, Heather Small's Proud, that accompanied him in the preparation phase of the Olympic Games.
Desert Island Discs is broadcast on BBC Radio 4 at 11:15 BST on Sunday.
Mickelson's low-in-a-shape stayed on the bench in the Ryder Cup on Saturday
American Phil Mickelson will set a record on Sunday when he plays his 47th Ryder Cup game, but will have to reverse his form if he is to prevent him from becoming a woeful milestone.
Mickelson, who played in the two-year-and-a-team event to secure a 12-game scoring, was knocked out by captain Furyk for Saturday's four-day-double.
Rather than being at the center of the action, as so many times he's done for the U.S., the five-time major tournament champion had to settle for cheering in a few moments and, in others, trying to perfect his game on the pitch and thus be able to rectify what afflicts him.
Never the most direct of drivers, not even at the height of his career, the 48-year-old player isn't in his best form for the Le Golf National field, where the long-running rough continually chastles with misdemeanors.
And if the field is no longer daunting enough, Mickelson, in Sunday's ninth game, faces brilliant British Open champion Francesco Molinari, who has joined debutant Tommy Fleetwood in winning the four matches they have played this week.
If the Americans, who were four points lower at the start of 12 individual games, booted impressively, Mickelson's game could have been decisive.
Furyk showed confidence in his mate, he couldn't say much else either.
"He perfectly understood the role I had today, he patted me on the back and, surrounding myself with his arm, said he would be ready tomorrow," Furyk said.
"She's very self-confident.
He's in the Hall of Fame and he's contributed a lot to these teams before and this week.
Probably wouldn't imagine him playing two games.
I thought it'd be more, but that's how it came out and that's how we thought it had to be.
He wants to be there, like the rest".
Mickelson will surpass Nick Faldo's mark of more Ryder Cup-game games on Sunday.
It could be the latest of an entire Ryder Cup trajectory who had never so clearly matched the levels of his individual brand.
Mickelson has 18 wins, 20 loss and seven equal, though Furyk said his presence brought many more intangible values to the team.
"It's fun, sarcastic, ingenious, he likes to mock each other; it's great to have him on the team," he explained.
"I think the youngest players have loved to have him as a support, as well this week; it was fun.
It brings a lot more than just game."
The European captain, Thomas Bjorn, knows that the leadership can disappear very soon
Thomas Bjorn, Europe's cap-captain, is well aware that, even if you come in with a substantial advantage in the final days of the Ryder Cup, it can easily become an uncomfortable tournament.
The Dane made his debut in the 1997 match in Valderrama, where a side captained by Seve Ballesteros maintained a five-point advantage over the Americans, but in the end they only managed to put an end to the others by peeling their heels by a minimum margin, winning 141-131-2.
"You don't stop remembering that we did a great tournament in Valderrama; we also did it in Brookline and we lost, we did it in Valderrama and we won, but by very little," said Bjorn, portrayed, after watching the 2018 generation win 5-3 on Friday and yesterday lead him with a 10-6 at Le Golf National.
History will teach me and everyone that this team isn't finished.
You've got to give it all away tomorrow.
You go out there and do what you need to do.
This doesn't end until the dots are on the scoring.
We've got a goal to meet, which is to win this trophy, and that's all we have to think about.
I say it all the time, I'm focused on the 12 players of our team, but we're very aware of what's on the other side where the best players in the world are, no more or less."
Proud of what his players have done on a very tough golf course, Bjorn added: "I wouldn't want to get ahead.
Tomorrow is a different challenge.
What prevails tomorrow are the individual performances and this is different.
It's cool to share what's going on with a mate when things are going well, but when you're there alone, you test all your golfer ability.
That's the message you have to convey to the players, to get the best out of yourself tomorrow.
Now it's your clerk to put aside your mate, go and get the best out of yourself."
Facing Bjorn, his counterpart Jim Furyk will try to get his players to make it better individually than they did as a pair, with the exception Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas adding three points out of four.
Furyk himself has been at both ends in the last few days of those big tournaments and has been part of the winning team in Brookline and then ended up losing when Europe held the "Miracle of Medinah."
"I remember every word of it," he told us when asked how Ben Crenshaw, the captain in 1999, managed to motivate his players on the last day.
"We've got 12 super-important games tomorrow, but we've got to rip off as well as we've done in Brookline, as you've glimpsed at Medinah.
When we're all moving in one direction, all the pressure lies with those mid-term parties.
We defined our strategy and got them out with the feeling that we were going to try to do something magical tomorrow."
Thomas is tasked with trying to lead the fight and face Rory McIlroy in the main game, with Paul Casey, Justin Rose, Jon Rahm, Tommy Fleetwood and Ian Poulter, the other Europeans in the second half on the list.
"I went with this bunch of guys in this order because I think they'll cover the entire tour," Bjorn said of his individual selections.
Germany's new warship deferred again
Germany's new Navy frigate should have been tasked in 2014 to replace the old Cold War-era ships, but it won't be done until at least next year due to defective systems and a progressive increase in costs, local media reported.
The delivery of the "Rheinland-Pfalz," the ship that heads the new frigates of the Baden-W.rttemberg class, has now been postponed until the first half of 2019, according to statements by a military spokesman Die Zeit.
The ship should have joined the Navy in 2014, but the problems that arose after the handover screwed the completion of the ambitious project.
The four Baden-Wuerttemberg-class ships requested by the Navy in 2007 will replace the old Bremen-class frigates.
We know that they will include a powerful canyon, a set of anti-aircraft and anti-ship missiles, as well as some invisibility technologies such as radar, infrared signals and very small acoustics.
Other important features include longer periods of maintenance, so that they can be deployed for up to two years away from ports of origin.
Continued delays, however, have caused the innovators - which were supposed to allow Germany to project its power overseas - to be outdated when they start serving, Die Zeit says.
Unfortunsary frigate F125 made headliners last year, when the German navy officially refused to put the ship into operation and returned it to Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg.
It's the first time the Navy has returned a boat to a yard after its delivery.
Little was known of the reasons behind the devolution, but the German media cited a number of crucial "supp software and hardware defects" that would leave the ship virtually useless if it was sent to a combat mission.
Deficiencies in software were particularly important, as Baden-Wuerttemberg-class ships will be operated by a crew of about 120 sailors - half of the staff in the former Bremen-class frigates.
It was also found that the ship is overheated, which reduces its performance and limits the Navy's ability to add future updates.
It's believed the 7,000-ton "Rheinland-Pfalz" is believed to be twice as heavy as other similar-class ships the Germans wore in World War II.
In addition to defraucous hardware, the price of the entire project, including the training of the crew, has also been a problem.
It is believed to have reached $3.1 billion ($3.6 billion), rather than the initial $22.00 million.
The problems attributed to the new frigates are of particular relevance in the light of recent warnings about the reduction of Germany's naval power.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, head of Germany's parliament's defence committee, acknowledged that the navy is "sleeping without ships capable of deploying."
The official said the problem has worsened over time, because the old boats were decommissioned, but haven't been replaced with others.
He lamented that neither Baden-Wuerttemberg's-class fragrants were able to join the Navy.
National Trust spies the secret life of batsmen
This new research, which has been carried out in the Scottish Highlands state, tries to figure out how batsmen use the landscape in their quest for food.
It's expected that everything that can be found will shed more light on the behavior of the only flying mammals and help to guide the upcoming conservation activities.
The study, carried out by the National Trust for Scotland, will follow common and soprano bats, as well as Daubenton coastal bats and long-eight-eyed browns from Inverewe Gardens in Wester Ross.
Special recorders will be positioned in strategic locations of the property to track the activities of the bats during the season.
NHS staff and volunteers will also carry out mobile research with laptop detectors.
Analysts will look at the sounds collected in the footage to discover the frequency of bat calls and what species they do.
It will then produce a report and a map of the habitat to create a detailed picture of the environment and its behavior.
Rob Dewar, nature's conservation adviser for NTS, hopes the results will rediscover which areas of the habitat are most important for bats and how each species uses them.
This information will determine the benefits of habitat management work, such as the creation of meadows and how forests should be maintained for bats and other associated species.
Bat populations in Scotland and the United Kingdom have declined considerably over the last century.
They are under threat because of the construction and development that affects their nests and the loss of their habitat.
Wind turbines and electrical cabling can also pose a risk to them, as well as insecticide papers and other chemical treatments of construction materials or attacks by domestic cats.
The bats aren't really blind.
"But because of your night-hunting habits, your ears are more useful than your eyes to achieve your goal.
They use a sophisticated e-localization technique that locates ants and hurdles in their flight path.
The NTS, which is responsible for caring for more than 270 historic buildings, 38 important gardens and 76,000 hectares of land across the country, takes the issue of bats very seriously.
It has ten experts trained in this area, who are dedicated to regularly carrying out surveys, nest inspections and sometimes ransoms.
The organisation has even unveiled Scotland's first-and-on-a-studded reserve in Threave State in Dumfries and Galloway, which is home to eight of Scotland's ten bat species.
Property manager David Thompson says it's ideal territory for them.
"Here in Threave, we've got a fantastic area for bat," he said.
"We've got old buildings, a lot of veteran trees and all the right habitat.
However, there are still plenty of things unknown about the bats, so the work we do here and elsewhere will help us better understand what they need to develop."
It emphasizes the importance of verifying whether there are bats before carrying out maintenance tasks inside the properties, since a breeding colony can involuntarily be unintentionally destroyed and 400 females and offspring could be killed, which would mean annihilating an entire local population.
The bats are protected and it is illegal to kill them, harass or annoy them, as well as destroy their colonies.
Elisabeth Ferrell, the Scottish officer in charge of the Bat Conservation Trust, has encouraged the public to collaborate.
He said: "We've still got a lot to learn about bats and many other species we don't know how their populations evolve."
Ronaldo denies the rape allegations at the same time that his lawyers persecute the German magazine
Cristiano Ronaldo has branded the rape allegations "fake news," saying people "wants to make themselves known" at the expense of his name.
His lawyers have been instructed to denounce the German newspaper Der Spiegel, which is where the statements were published.
The striker of Portugal and Juventus is accused of raping an U.S. woman named Kathryn Mayorga at a Las Vegas hotel in 2009.
He is accused of being paid $355,000 to hide the incident, Der Spiegel reported on Friday.
Using an Instagram Live video that has already racked up 142 million followers a few hours after the news was published, Ronaldo, 33, described the report as "fake news."
"No, no, no, no, no.
What they've said today is fake," says the five-time Golden Balcony winner looking at the camera.
"They only want publicity at the expense of my name.
It's the same story as always.
They wanna become celebrities just by mentioning me, but that's right, I cann't avoid it.
I'm a Happy Man and everything's okay," the player added, smiling.
Ronaldo's lawyers are preparing a lawsuit against Der Spiegel over the allegations in what they have called "an unacceptable statement of suspicion in the area of privacy," according to Reuters reports.
barrister Christian Schertz said that the player will seek compensation for "moral damages for an amount corresponding to the seriousness of the violation, which is probably one of the most serious breaches of his personal rights in recent years."
The alleged incident reportedly took place in June 2009 at a suite at the Palms Hotel and Casino in Las Vegas.
After they met in a nightclub, Ronaldo and Mayorga allegedly went to the player's room and there he allegedly raped her anally, according to documents recorded in the Clark County District Court in Nevada.
Mayorga said Ronaldo knelt after the alleged incident and told him it was "99 per cent" a "good kid" and that he had been disappointed that "one per per cent" remaining.
The documents state that Ronaldo confirmed that the couple had sex, but that he was consented to.
Mayorga also claims that she went to the police who took photographs of the wounds in the hospital, but later agreed to reach an out-of-court settlement because she had a "terror of reprisals" and was concerned about "being humiliated in public."
The 34-year-year-old woman says she's trying to override the deal as she's still traumatized by the facts.
When the alleged assault took place, Ronaldo was about to join Real Madrid from Manchester United and this summer has switched to Juve, an Italian soccer great for a contract of $100 million.
Brexit: The UK would always regret losing carmakers
The United Kingdom "would remorse all our lives" if it lost its position as a world leader in car manufacturing after Brexit, Industry Secretary Greg Clark said.
He added it was "concerning" that Toyota UK informed the BBC that if Britain left the EU without an agreement, production would be temporarily halted at its Burnaston factory near Derby.
"We need an agreement," Mr. Clark said.
The Japanese automaker said the impact of delays at customs in the event of an unintended Brexit could lead to the loss of jobs.
The Burnaston plant, which manufactures Toyota Auris and Avensis models, produced about 150,000 cars last last year, of which 90 per cent was exported to the rest of the European Union.
"My opinion is, if Britain breaks with the EU at the end of March, there will be production stops in our factory," said Marvin Cooke, Toyota's managing director in Burnaston.
Other U.S. carmakers have expressed fears about leaving the EU without an agreement, particularly in relation to cross-border trade, including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover.
BMW, for example, says it will shut down its Mini plant in Oxford a month after Brexit.
The main worries referred to by carmakers revolve around the problems that could arise in the assembly chains in the event of a Brexit without agreement.
Toyota &apos; s production line is based on the adjusted production mode (just in time or at the last moment), where the parts arrive every 37 minutes from suppliers in the United Kingdom and the EU to manufacture on-demand cars.
If the United Kingdom leaves the European Union on 29 March without an agreement, there could be an interruption at the border that, according to the sector, could lead to delays and shortages of parts.
For Toyota it would be impossible to accommodate the amount of material equivalent to more than a one-day inventory at its Derbyshire plant, the company said, and, in that case, the production would be halted.
Mr. Clark said Theresa May's Chequers scheme, where future relations with the EU are established, is "precisely crazy to avoid such controls on border crossings."
"We need an agreement - we want to have the best agreement that will not only allow us to get out of the way at the moment but we must seize the opportunity," he said on BBC Radio 4's Today programme.
"Toyota's evidence, among many other manufacturers, is that we need to be able to continue what, so far, has been a tremendously successful set of supply chains."
Toyota hasn't been able to say how long the production would be halted, but he cautioned that, in the long term, the added costs would reduce the plant's competitiveness and ultimately the costs.
Peter Tsouvallaris, who has worked in Burnaston for 24 years and is in charge of the Unite syndicate at the plant, said that his members are increasingly concerned: "Depending on my inexperience, once those jobs are lost, they are never recovering."
A government spokesman said: "We have put in place an accurate and convincing plan outlining our future relations with the EU."
Trump's meeting with Rosenstein could be postponed again, according to the White House
Donald Trump's high-risk meeting with Deputy Attorney-General Rod Rosenstein could "get another week," as the dispute over Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh continues, the White House said on Sunday.
Rosenstein supervises the work of special adviser Robert Mueller, who is investigating interference in the Russian election, links between Trump's aid and Russia, and a possible obstruction of justice by the president.
Expectation as to whether Trump decides to ospiel the vice-prosecutor-to-General or not, and thus compromising Mueller's independence has fueled Washington's gossip for months.
Earlier this month, the New York Times reported that Rosenstein recorded conversations with Trump with a microphone that he himself wore to try to defy the president through the 25th amendment.
Rosenstein denied it was true.
Last Monday, however, he went to the White House, amid news reports said he was about to resign.
Instead, a meeting with Trump (who at the time was at the United Nations in New York) was announced on Thursday.
Trump "would rather not" fire Rosenstein, but the meeting had to be postponed to avoid a conflict with the U.S. Senate Judiciary Committee hearing in which Kavanaugh and one of the women accusing him of sexual assault, Dr. Christine Blasey Ford, testified.
On Friday, Trump asked the F.B.I. to investigate the allegations against Kavanaugh for a week, although that would mean delaying the full Senate vote.
Trump's press secretary, Sarah Sanders, appeared Sunday on Fox News.
Asked about Rosenstein's reunion, he said: "It hasn't defined any date, it could be this week or another could be postponed, considering what's going on with the Supreme Court.
We'll be deciding on the fly and I'm gonna report to the press, as I usually do.'
Some reporters would refute that claim: Sanders hasn't held a press conference at the White House since September 10.
presenter Chris Wallace asked what the reasons were.
Sanders said the paucity of press conferences wasn't due to an aversion to TV reporters acting petty, though he said: "I won't disagree with the fact they bluff."
She then suggested that direct contact between Trump and the press is going to increase.
"The president conducts more question-and-answer sessions than any previous president," he said, while quoting without evidence: "We've contrasted those numbers."
The press conferences will continue, Sanders said, but "If the press has the option of directly asking the President of the United States, I think it's a lot better than making it to me.
We've been trying to do it many times and you've seen us do it many times in the last few weeks, so this would replace a press conference when you can talk to the President of the United States."
Trump usually answers questions when he leaves the White House or when he participates in public sessions or press conferences with dignitaries who visit him.
The press conferences with him only happen on very rare occasions.
