Earthquakes and Tsunami in Indonesia
At least 384 people have been killed by a powerful earthquake and tsunami that plagued the Indonesian city of Palu last Friday, authorities said. The death toll is expected to increase.
With communications blocked, aid officials have not been able to obtain any information about the Donggala Regency, an area north of Palu that lies closer to the epicenter of the magnitude 7.5 earthquake.
More than 16,000 people were evacuated in Palu after the disaster.
Here you can see some important details about Palu and Donggala on Sulawesi Isle:
Palu is the capital of the Central Sulawesi province, which sits at the end of a narrow bay on the west shore of Sulawesi Island with an estimated 379,800 population in 2017.
The city was celebrating its 40th anniversary when the Earthquake and Tsunami took place.
Donggala is a requisiency that extends more than 300 km (180 miles) of coastline in the north-north island of Sulawesi.
The regency, which is a hierarchically lower administrative region than the province, had an estimated population of 299,200 in 2017.
Fisheries and agriculture are the pillars of the economy of Central Sulawesi province, especially in the Donggala coastal region.
The nickel mines are also of importance in the province, but are mainly concentrated in Morowali on the opposite coast of Sulawesi.
Palu and Donggala have been affected by tsunami several times over the past 100 years, according to Indonesia's Disaster Mitigation Agency.
In 1938, a tsunami killed more than 200 people and destroyed hundreds of houses in Donggala.
Tsunami also battered western Donggala in 1996, with nine fatalities.
Indonesia is located in the Pacific Bel Belt of Fire, an area of great seismic activity, and it often suffers earthquakes frequently.
Below are some of the major quakes and tsunamis in recent years:
2004: A major earthquake on the west coast of Indonesia's Aceh province in northern Sumatra on December 26 triggered a tsunami that affected 14 countries and claimed the lives of 226,000 people on the Indian Ocean coast. More than half of the casualties occurred in Aceh.
2005: A series of strong earthquakes hit the West Sumatra shore in late March and in early April.
Hundreds of fatalities occurred on the island of Nias, off the coast of Sumatra.
2006: A 6.8-magnitude quake ravaged southern Java, Indonesia &apos; steaper-populated island, sparking a tsunami affecting the southern streak with nearly 700 fatalities.
2009: A 7.6-magnitude quake struck near Padang city, the capital of West Sumatra province.
More than 1,100 people were killed.
2010: A 7.5-magnitude quake battered one of the Mentawai Islands, near Sumatra, sparking a tsunami of up to 10-footer that shattered dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
2016: A shallow quake hit Pidie Jaya regency in Aceh, causing destruction and panic as it recalls the devastation of the deadly 2004 earthquake and tsunami.
This time it didn't cause a tsunami, but the collapse of buildings killed more than 100 people.
2018: The tourist island of Lombok (Indonesia) had a series of high-magnitude quakes that killed more than 500 people, mostly on the northern side of the island.
The quake devastated thousands of buildings and thousands of tourists were temporarily evicted.
The eldest son of Sarah Palin Arrested on Domestic Violence Charges
Track Palin, the eldest son of the former governor of Alaska and candidate for Vice President Sarah Palin, has been arrested on assault charges.
Palin, a 29-year-old citizen of Wasilla (Alaska), was arrested on suspicion of domestic violence, interfering with a report of domestic violence and resisting arrest, according to a report released Saturday by Alaska State Police.
According to the police report, when his acquaintance tried to call the police to report the alleged crimes, he took off his phone.
Palin has been remanded in pre-trial detention at Mat-Su Pretrial Facility and is being held on an unsecured $500 bail, KTUU reported.
He appeared before the court on Saturday, where he pleaded "definitely innocent" when his statement was asked, the network reported.
Palin is facing three Class A misdemeanor, which means she could spend up to a year in jail and receive a $250,000 fine.
He has also been charged with a Class B misdemeanour with a one-day prison sentence and a $2,000 fine.
This isn't the first time there's been criminal charges against Palin.
In December 2017, he was accused of assaulting his father, Todd Palin.
His mother, Sarah Palin, called police to report the alleged attack.
The case is currently before the Alaska Veterans Court.
In January 2016, he was charged with domestic assault, interfering with reporting a domestic violence offence and possessing a weapon in a state of drunkenness in relation to the incident.
His girlfriend had claimed he punched him in the face.
Several groups of veterans criticised Sarah Palin in 2016 for tying her son's violent behavior to post-traumatic stress disorder stemming from his service in Iraq.
