U.S. prosecutors exceptionally seek the death penalty for New NY . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
New York's federal prosecutors are searching for capital punishment for Sayfullo Saipov, the suspected New York City terror attack that killed eight people, an exceptional punishment that hasn't been executed in the state for a federal offense since 1953.
Saipov's 30-year-and-minisoul-year-old Saipov allegedly used a Home Depot rental van to carry out a bike lane last October on the Lower Manhattan's West Side Highway knocking down pedestrians and cyclists on his way.
To justify a death sentence, prosecutors will have to prove that Saipov "intentionally" killed the eight victims and "intentionally caused grievous bodily harm," in accordance with the warning of the intention to apply for the death penalty, filed in the Southern District of New York.
The two counts carry a possible death sentence, according to the court document.
Several weeks after the attack, a federal grand judrant presented Saipov with a 22-accurate indictment that included eight counts of manslaughter with the help of organized crime, often used by federal prosecutors in such cases, and one count of violence and destruction of motorized vehicles.
The attack required substantial planning and premeditation, according to the prosecutors, who described how Saipov carried it out as "atrocious, cruel and depraved."
"Sayfullo Habibullaevic Saipov caused injuries, damage and loss to the families and friends of Diego Enrique Angelini, Nicholas Cleves, Ann-Laure Decadt, Darren Drake, Ariel Erlij, Hernan Ferruchi, Hernan Diego Mendoza and Alejandro Damian Pagnucco," is specified in the statement of intent.
Five of the victims were tourists from Argentina.
It's only been a decade since the Southern District of New York last processed a death penalty case.
Defendant Khalid Barnes pleaded not guilty to murdering two drug dealers but was eventually sentenced to life in prison in September 2009.
The last time a death toll was carried out in a federal case in New York went to Julius and Ethel Rosenberg in 1953. This marriage was executed after they were convicted of conspiracy to commit espionage for the Soviet Union during the Cold War two years earlier.
The two Rosenbergs were executed in the electric chair on June 19, 1953.
Saipov, an immigrant originally from Uzbekistan, demonstrated a lack of remorse in the days and months after the attack, according to court documents.
According to the police, he told investigators he felt good about what he had done.
According to the accusation, Saipov told the authorities that for his attack he was inspired by watching ISIS videos on his phone.
He also requested that the ISIS banner be hoisted in his hospital room, police said.
He has pleaded not guilty to a 22-count indictment.
David Patton, one of the federal public defense attorneys representing Saipov, said they are opaciously disappointed with the prosecutor's decision.
"We believe that the decision to seek the death penalty rather than accepting a guilty plea with a life sentence without the possibility of his release will only prolong the trauma of these events for everyone involved," Patton said.
Saipov &apos; s defense team had previously asked prosecutors not to seek the death penalty.
