North Korean defector's Defector's Defection Shows With Kim
The number of defectors from North Korea to South Korea has decreased since Kim Jong-un came to power seven years ago, a South Korean lawmaker has said.
Park Byeong-seug, quoting data from the Southern Unification Ministry, said there were 1,127 defections last year, compared to 2,706 in 2011.
Park said tighter border controls between North Korea and China, and the high fares charged by human smugglers have been the key factors.
Pyongyang has made no public comment on the matter.
The vast majority of defectors in the North are finally offered South Korean citizenship.
Seoul says more than 30,000 North Koreans have crossed the border illegally since the end of the Korean War in 1953.
The majority flee across China, which has the longest border with North Korea and is easier to cross than the heavily-protected Demilitarized Zone (DMZ).
China sees defectors as illegal immigrants instead of refugees and are often repatriated.
Relations between the North and the South, which are still technically at war, have improved markedly in recent months.
Earlier this month, the leaders of the two countries gathered in Pyongyang for talks that focused on denuclearization negotiations.
This came after the historic meeting in June between U.S. President Donald Trump and Kim Jong-un in Singapore, who broadly agreed to work on a nuclear-weapon-free Korean peninsula.
But on Saturday, North Korea's Foreign Minister Ri Yong-ho blasted U.S. U.S. sanctions for lack of progress.
"Without U.S. trust, there will be no confidence in our national security and, under these circumstances, in no way we will be the first to disarm unilaterally," Ri said in a speech to the U.N. General Assembly in New York.
Nancy Pelosi Calls Brett Kavanaugh Hythetic and Says He's Unfit to Serve in the Supreme Court
Nancy Pelosi, the minority leader of the House of Representatives, said Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh was an "hysterical" and that he was not fit to serve in the Supreme Court.
Pelosi made these comments in an interview Saturday at the Texas Tribune Festival in Austin, Texas.
"I can't help but think that if a woman had acted like that, they would have said that she is a hysterical," Pelosi said about his reaction to Kavanaugh's testimony Thursday before the Senate Judiciary Committee.
Kavanaugh lamented emotion to deny the allegations that she had sexually assaulted doctor Christine Blasey Ford when both were teenagers.
During his initial statement, Kavanaugh was very excited, at times almost screaming and choking as he talked about his family and years of high school.
On the committee, he also explicitly condemned the Democrats , citing the accusations against them as grotesque and clearly coordinated defamations - organized by angry liberals because Hillary Clinton lost the 2016 presidential election.
Pelosi said Kavanaugh's testimony in her opinion proved she couldn't serve in the Supreme Court, because it showed her bias against Democrats.
"I think he disqualifies himself from those statements with and the way he has persecuted the Clintons and the Democrats," he said.
Pelosi raised her objections when she was asked if she would challenge Kavanaugh in the event that she was confirmed in office and that Democrats obtained a majority in the House of Representatives.
"I'll only say one thing: if you're not telling the truth to Congress or the FBI, you won't be fit not only to be on the Supreme Court, it won't even be there to be in the court in which you hold now," Pelosi said.
Kavanaugh is currently a judge on the U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals in Washington, D.C.
Pelosi added that, as a Democrat, she was concerned about Kavanaugh's possible sentences against the Af Affordable Care Act or the Roe Against Wade case because she is considered to be a Conservative judge.
At his confirmation hearings, Kavanaugh avoided questions about whether he would override certain Supreme Court decisions.
"That's not the time for a hysterical, impartial person to go to the court and wait for us to think everything's okay," Pelosi said.
