U.S.-China trade war could benefit Beijing
The first saviurs of the trade war between the U.S. and China were deafening, and while there's still plenty for the battle to end, a rift between the countries could benefit Beijing, experts say.
U.S. President Donald Trump threw the first warning earlier this year by imposing tariffs on major Chinese exports, such as solar panels, steel and aluminum.
The most significant escalation took place this week with new tariffs affecting $200 billion ($150bn) on products, which carries taxes on half of the products entering the United States from China.
On all occasions, Beijing has retaliated equivalently, recently applying tariffs of five to 10 per per cent on U.S. products worth $60 billion.
China has vowed to equate U.S. attacks, and the world's second-largest economy is unlikely to loosen soon.
Getting Washington to back down would mean relinquishing the lawsuits, but submitting publicly to the U.S. would be too embarrassing for Chinese President Xi Jinping.
Still, pundits say that if Beijing plays its cards well, the pressures of the U.S. trade war could positively help China in the long term by reducing the interdependence between the two economies.
"The fact that a quick political decision in Washington or in Beijing can cause conditions to start a plummeting of the economy in either country is actually much more dangerous than viewers have acknowledged so far," said Abigail Grace, an associate researcher focused on Asian issues for the Strategic Cabinet Center for New American Security.
Syria poised for refugees to return, foreign minister says
Syria says it is poised for the voluntary return of the refugees and is seeking help in rebuilzing a war devastated country for more than seven years.
In an earlier speech at the U.N. General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem said the country's conditions were improving.
"The situation on the ground today is more stable and secure thanks to the progress that has been achieved in the fight against terrorism," he said.
The government is still rehabilitating areas destroyed by the terrorists to try to restore normalcy.
Already, there are all the conditions for the voluntary return of refugees who had to leave the country because of terrorism and the unilateral economic measures that affected their daily lives and livelihoods.
The U.N. estimates that, since the war began in 2011, more than 5.5 million Syrians have fled the country.
A further six million people are still living in the country and in need of humanitarian aid.
Al-Moualem has acknowledged that the Syrian regime would welcome help to rebuke the devastated country.
But he also emphasized that he would not accept conditional aid or the help of the countries that promoted the insurgency.
Europe takes over the Ryder Cup in Paris
The European team has won the 2018 Ryder Cup by beating the U.S. team with a final score of 16.5 to 10.5 at Le Golf National, on the outskirts of Paris (France).
The U.S. now carries six consecutive defeats on European soil since the last winning an Ryder Cup in Europe in 1993.
Europe regained the knock-wheel-drive when Danish captain Thomas Bjorn's team achieved the 14.5 points needed to beat the U.S.
American star Phil Mickelson, who had to fight for most of the tournament, threw his starting point into the water on the 16-under-par 3, thus admitting his defeat to Francesco Molinari.
The Italian golfer Molinari excelled in all his rounds, becoming one of the four players who have never achieved a 5-0-0 since he began the current format of the tournament in 1979.
The American Jordan Spieth was defeated by 5 and 4 against the player with the lowest ranking of the European team, the Danish Thorbjorn Olesen.
The world's number one player, Dustin Johnson, fell 2 and 1 to Englishman Ian Poulter, who could have played his last Ryder Cup.
The Spaniard Sergio Garcia, a veteran who already has eight Ryder Cups, became the European with the most victories of the tournament of all time, with 25.5 points in his career.
