Red tide update: Concentrations in Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota decline
The new report from the Florida Fish and Flora Fish and Wildlife Commission shows an overall reduction in red tide rallies in Tampa Bay area.
Irregular blooming conditions are being noticed, according to the FWC in areas of Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier counties, suggesting a reduction in rallies.
The red tide bloom extends about 130 miles from Pinellas County north to Lee County to the south.
Irrigated pitches can be found about 10 miles off the Hillsborough County coast, but at fewer places when compared to last week.
Red tide has also been detected in Pasco County.
Over the past week, average concentrations have been reported in Pinellas County and its shore, concentrations between lows and highs on the Hillsborough County coast, bottom-to-high concentrations in Manatee County, bottom-to-high concentrations in Sarasota County and its coast, background-to-medium rallies in Charlotte's County, deep-to-privity rallies rallies in Lee County and its shore, and low concentrations in Collier County.
Respiratory irritation problems are still being reported in Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier counties.
Respiratory irritation problems have not been reported in northwest Florida over the past week.
